From the stages of Saigon to the lights of America, Thùy Vân is a force that refuses to be contained by a single art form.

She started young. Ballet at seven. Violin alongside it. By eleven, she'd won a gold medal in tango at a national competition in Saigon — the kind of kid who doesn't just learn art, but lives inside it.

At eighteen she moved to the United States, where she became a champion in American and international dancesport competitions. But dancing was never the whole story. Thùy Vân is a singer, a host, a performer who can hold a ten-hour show across multiple roles and make it look effortless.

She's been a cornerstone of Minh Chánh Entertainment, one of the most prominent Vietnamese entertainment centers in the diaspora. And she came back. Six weeks after a C-section, she returned to the stage — singing, dancing, hosting — because that's who she is.

Her music bridges worlds. "Pray" is an English-language track that carries the emotional weight of Vietnamese vocal tradition into a global sound. It's a beginning. Through Timbre, every song becomes six songs — reimagined across genres, cultures, and audiences that haven't heard her yet.

The world is about to.
